Presidential elections were held in [[Slovenia]] in October and November 2007 to elect the successor to [[Janez Drnovšek]].<ref>{{Cite web |url=http://english.people.com.cn/90001/90777/6220791.html |title=Slovenian presidential election to be held in October |publisher=Xinhua |date=20 July 2007 |access-date=2008-09-21 |archive-date=8 October 2012 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20121008005714/http://english.people.com.cn/90001/90777/6220791.html |url-status=live }}</ref> [[France Cukjati]], the President of the [[National Assembly (Slovenia)|National Assembly]], called the elections on 20 June 2007.<ref>{{Cite web|url= http://24ur.com/novice/slovenija/volitve-cez-tri-mesece.html|title= Volitve čez tri mesece|access-date= 2008-09-05|publisher= 24ur.com|date= 20 June 2007|language= sl|archive-date= 20 May 2011|archive-url= https://web.archive.org/web/20110520151334/http://24ur.com/novice/slovenija/volitve-cez-tri-mesece.html|url-status= live}}</ref>

Seven candidates competed in the first round on 21 October 2007;<ref>{{Cite web |url=http://24ur.com/bin/article.php?article_id=3106278 |title=Sedem kandidatov na volitve |access-date=2008-09-05 |publisher=24ur.com |date=27 September 2007 |language=sl |archive-date=1 July 2012 |archive-url=https://archive.today/20120701102640/http://24ur.com/bin/article.php?article_id=3106278 |url-status=live }}</ref> three entered the race as independent candidates, the other four were supported by political parties. Several political events, as well as tension between the Government and the political opposition, overshadowed the campaign. The front runner [[Lojze Peterle]], supported by the governing conservative coalition, won the first round with far fewer votes than predicted by opinion polls. In the second round, held on 11 November 2007, Peterle faced the runner-up, the left-wing candidate [[Danilo Türk]]. Türk won the second round in a landslide, with 68.03% of the vote.<ref name="vp2007">{{Cite web|url= http://volitve.gov.si/vp2007/|title= Volitve predsednika republike 2007|access-date= 2008-09-05|date= 11 November 2007|language= sl|url-status= dead|archive-url= https://web.archive.org/web/20080706141939/http://volitve.gov.si/vp2007/|archive-date= 6 July 2008}}</ref>

In a referendum called by the [[National Council (Slovenia)|National Council]], and held on the same day as the second round, the electorate voted to overturn a law providing for the nationalization of citizens' share in the major national insurance company. Nearly three-quarters of the votes were cast against the law.<ref name="vp2007" /> After both election and referendum results were announced, [[Prime Minister of Slovenia|Prime Minister]] [[Janez Janša]] announced that he might resign, following what he perceived to be a heavy defeat for the government. The government later won a [[Motion of Confidence|vote of confidence]] in the National Assembly.

==Background==
[[File:Janez Drnovsek.jpg|left|150px|thumb|[[Janez Drnovšek]], the incumbent president, announced he would not run for president again]]

The role of the president of Slovenia is mainly ceremonial. One of the president's duties is to nominate the Prime Minister, after consulting with political groups represented in the [[National Assembly (Slovenia)|National Assembly]]. The president also proposes candidates for various state offices, as well as judicial appointments to the Constitutional and Supreme Court, which must be approved by the National Assembly. In rare circumstances, the president possesses the power to pass laws and dissolve the National Assembly. The President is also the Supreme Commander of the [[Military of Slovenia|Armed Forces]]. Unlike the majority of the government, which is chosen by the National Assembly and elected through [[proportional representation]], the president is directly elected by the majority of Slovenian voters.<ref>{{Cite web |url=http://www.dfat.gov.au/geo/slovenia/slovenia_brief.html |title=Slovenia Country Brief |access-date=2008-02-05 |publisher=Australian Government Department of Foreign Affairs and Trade |archive-date=20 January 2008 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20080120003813/http://www.dfat.gov.au/geo/slovenia/slovenia_brief.html |url-status=live }}</ref>

The [[2002 Slovenian presidential election|previous presidential election]] in 2002 brought major changes to [[politics of Slovenia|Slovenian politics]]. The former president [[Milan Kučan]], in office since the first free elections held in the [[Republic of Slovenia (1990–1991)|Republic of Slovenia]] in April 1990 (before the country's independence from [[Yugoslavia]]), was forbidden by the constitution from running for president again, and announced his retirement from active politics. Prime Minister Janez Drnovšek of the [[Liberal Democracy of Slovenia]] stood for the office, comfortably winning the [[Two-round system|runoff]] against conservative candidate [[Barbara Brezigar]].<ref name="RVK">{{Cite web|url= http://volitve.gov.si/vp2002/|title= Poročilo o izidu volitev predsednika republike|access-date= 2008-09-05|publisher= Republiška volilna komisija|date= 10 December 2007|language= sl|url-status= dead|archive-url= https://web.archive.org/web/20111010060126/http://volitve.gov.si/vp2002/|archive-date= 10 October 2011}}</ref>

The [[2004 Slovenian parliamentary election|2004 legislative election]] brought further changes and a political swing to the right. [[Janez Janša]], the leader of a right-wing coalition, formed the new government. In Slovenia, this was the first time after 1992 that the President and the Prime Minister had represented opposing political factions for more than a few months. Between 2002 and 2004, the relationship between President Drnovšek and Janez Janša, then leader of the opposition, were considered more than good<ref>{{Cite web|url=http://www.mladina.si/tednik/200328/clanek/konvencija/index.print.html-l2|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20030729023802/http://www.mladina.si/tednik/200328/clanek/konvencija/index.print.html-l2|url-status=dead|archive-date=29 July 2003|title=Iskalci vizije|access-date=2009-02-09|year=2003|publisher=Mladina|language=sl}}</ref> and in the first year of cohabitation, no major problems arose.

At the beginning of his term, Drnovšek, who was ill with cancer, stayed out of public view. When he reemerged in late 2005 he had changed his lifestyle: he became a [[vegan]], moved out of the capital into the countryside, and withdrew from party politics completely, ending his already frozen membership in the Liberal Democracy. Drnovšek's new approach to politics prompted one political commentator to nickname him "Slovenia's Gandhi".<ref name="NYT about Drnovšek">{{Cite news|url= https://www.nytimes.com/2006/09/09/world/europe/09drnovsek.html|title= Slovenian President Finds Peace and Wants to Share It|access-date= 2008-09-05|work= The New York Times|date= 9 September 2006|first= Nicholas|last= Wood|archive-date= 18 January 2016|archive-url= https://web.archive.org/web/20160118075148/http://www.nytimes.com/2006/09/09/world/europe/09drnovsek.html|url-status= live}}</ref><ref name="Times">{{cite news|url= http://women.timesonline.co.uk/tol/life_and_style/women/the_way_we_live/article2870371.ece|title= All hail the mystic President|access-date= 2008-09-05|newspaper= Times Online|date= 15 November 2007|location= London|first= Martin|last= Fletcher|archive-date= 2 December 2008|archive-url= https://web.archive.org/web/20081202053513/http://women.timesonline.co.uk/tol/life_and_style/women/the_way_we_live/article2870371.ece|url-status= dead}}</ref>

The relationship between Drnovšek and the government quickly became tense. Disagreements began with Drnovšek's initiatives in foreign politics, aimed at solving major foreign conflicts, including those in [[Darfur conflict|Darfur]] and [[Kosovo]].<ref name="Times" /> Initially, these initiatives were not openly opposed by the Prime Minister, but were criticized by the foreign minister [[Dimitrij Rupel]],<ref>{{Cite web |url=http://www.mladina.si/tednik/200544/clanek/slo--zunanja_politika-ali_h_zerdin/ |title=Kosovska bitka: Drnovšek proti Ruplu |access-date=2009-02-09 |publisher=Mladina |year=2005 |language=sl |archive-date=24 September 2008 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20080924170245/http://www.mladina.si/tednik/200544/clanek/slo--zunanja_politika-ali_h_zerdin/ |url-status=live }}</ref> Drnovšek's former collaborator and close political ally until 2004.<ref>{{Cite web|url= http://24ur.com/novice/slovenija/rop-rupel-se-je-odlocil-po-svoje.html?ar=|title= Rop: Rupel se je odločil po svoje|access-date= 2009-02-09|publisher= 24ur.com|date= 2004-06-26|language= sl|archive-date= 29 September 2011|archive-url= https://web.archive.org/web/20110929161526/http://24ur.com/novice/slovenija/rop-rupel-se-je-odlocil-po-svoje.html?ar=|url-status= live}}</ref> The disagreements moved to issues of domestic politics in October 2006, when Drnovšek publicly criticised the treatment of the [[Romani people|Romani]] family Strojans. The neighborhood had forced the Strojans to relocate, which in turn subjected them to police supervision and limitation of movement.<ref name="NYT about Drnovšek"/> The disagreements however escalated when the parliamentary majority repeatedly rejected President's candidates for the Governor of the [[Bank of Slovenia]], beginning with the rejection of incumbent [[Mitja Gaspari]].<ref>{{Cite web|url= http://www.ukom.gov.si/eng/slovenia/publications/slovenia-news/4274/4288/|title= Gaspari Three Votes Short of Winning Second Term|access-date=2008-09-05 |publisher= Government communication office |date=6 February 2007 |archive-url = https://web.archive.org/web/20080305035710/http://www.ukom.gov.si/eng/slovenia/publications/slovenia-news/4274/4288/ |archive-date = 5 March 2008}}</ref> The friction continued over the appointment of other state official nominees, including [[Constitutional Court of Slovenia|Constitutional Court]] judges. Although the President's political support suffered after his personal transformation, the polls nevertheless showed public backing of the President against an increasingly unpopular Government.<ref name="Times" /><ref>{{Cite web|url= http://www.mladina.si/tednik/200722/clanek/slo-tema--jure_trampus/|title= Lepotica in zver|access-date= 2008-09-05|publisher= Mladina|language= sl|archive-date= 31 October 2007|archive-url= https://web.archive.org/web/20071031084616/http://www.mladina.si/tednik/200722/clanek/slo-tema--jure_trampus/|url-status= live}}</ref> The tension reached its height in May 2007, when the newly appointed director of the Slovenian Secret Service [[Matjaž Šinkovec]] unclassified several documents from the period before 2004, revealing, among other, that Drnovšek had used secret funds for personal purposes between 2002 and 2004. The President reacted with a harsh criticism of the government's policies, accusing the ruling coalition of abusing its power for personal delegitimation<ref>{{Cite web |url=http://24ur.com/novice/slovenija/vlada-me-skusa-zlomiti.html |title=Vlada me skuša zlomiti |access-date=2009-02-09 |publisher=24ur.com |date=18 May 2007 |language=sl |archive-date=29 September 2011 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20110929161440/http://24ur.com/novice/slovenija/vlada-me-skusa-zlomiti.html |url-status=live }}</ref> and labeled the Prime Minister as "the leader of the negative guys".<ref>{{Cite web |url=http://www.dnevnik.si/novice/slovenija/306840 |title=Obletnica Dnevnikovega razkritja: Po letu dni afera Sova še vedno brez epiloga |access-date=2009-02-09 |publisher=Dnevnik |date=21 March 2008 |language=sl |quote=''Predsednik Drnovšek je takrat o Janši zapisal: "Kaj počnejo naši negativci? Njihov poglavar je ves užaljen, ker sem njegovo veličino užalil z nekaj kritičnimi pripombami. In zato se mora maščevati. Nagnal je vse svoje pomagače, da najdejo kaj slabega, kaj blata za metanje. In brskajo in iščejo, pri tem jih nič ne moti, če uničujejo državne institucije, da bi le kaj našli, da bo gospodar zadovoljen."'' |archive-date=21 September 2009 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20090921012512/http://www.dnevnik.si/novice/slovenija/306840 |url-status=live }}</ref> After years of speculation about his health and intentions, Janez Drnovšek announced in February 2007 that he would not run for president again.<ref>{{Cite web|url= http://www.rtvslo.si/modload.php?&c_mod=rnews&op=sections&func=read&c_menu=1&c_id=112536|title= Drnovšek ne bo znova kandidiral|access-date= 2008-09-05|publisher= Rtvslo.si|date= 26 June 2006|language= sl|archive-date= 20 June 2008|archive-url= https://web.archive.org/web/20080620175546/http://www.rtvslo.si/modload.php?&c_mod=rnews&op=sections&func=read&c_menu=1&c_id=112536|url-status= live}}</ref>

==Candidates==
===Requirements for candidacy===
Under Slovenian Election Law, candidates for president require support of either:
* 10 members of the National Assembly,
* one or more political parties and either 3 members of the National Assembly or 3,000 voters,
* or 5,000 voters.

Each political party can support only one candidate. In the election, the president is elected with a majority of the vote. If no candidate receives more than half of votes, the top two candidates meet in the second round of election.<ref>{{Cite web|url= http://www.dvk.gov.si/PREDSEDNIK2007/ZVPR.html|title= Zakon o volitvah predsednika republike (ZVPR)|access-date= 2008-09-05|publisher= Uradni list RS, št. 39/1992|date= 7 August 1992|language= sl|archive-date= 5 September 2008|archive-url= https://web.archive.org/web/20080905112530/http://www.dvk.gov.si/PREDSEDNIK2007/ZVPR.html|url-status= live}}</ref>

===Leading candidates===
The first official candidate was [[Lojze Peterle]], a [[European People's Party|conservative]] member of the [[European Parliament]] and first democratically elected Prime Minister of Slovenia (1990–1992), who announced his candidacy in November 2006.<ref>{{Cite web|url= http://www.rtvslo.si/modload.php?&c_mod=rnews&op=sections&func=read&c_menu=37&c_id=124555|title= Peterle kandidat za predsednika države|access-date= 2008-09-05|publisher= Rtvslo.si|date= 2 November 2006|language= sl|archive-date= 6 May 2008|archive-url= https://web.archive.org/web/20080506142033/http://www.rtvslo.si/modload.php?&c_mod=rnews&op=sections&func=read&c_menu=37&c_id=124555|url-status= live}}</ref> He was endorsed by the three government center-right parties, [[New Slovenia]] (NSi), [[Slovenian Democratic Party]] (SDS), and [[Slovenian People's Party]] (SLS).<ref>{{Cite web|url= http://24ur.com/novice/slovenija/enotna-podpora-peterletu.html|title= Enotna podpora Peterletu|access-date= 2008-09-05|publisher= 24ur.com|date= 15 June 2007|language= sl|archive-date= 20 May 2011|archive-url= https://web.archive.org/web/20110520151437/http://24ur.com/novice/slovenija/enotna-podpora-peterletu.html|url-status= live}}</ref>

Drnovšek's announcement that he would not run for president again led to expectations that the [[Social Democrats (Slovenia)|Social Democrats]] (SD) would nominate their leader [[Borut Pahor]] and indeed Pahor confirmed that he was ready to run for the office.<ref>{{Cite web|url=http://www.finance.si/178684/|title=Pahor pripravljen kandidirati za predsednika države|access-date=2008-09-05|publisher=Finance|date=31 March 2007|language=sl|archive-date=18 January 2016|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20160118075148/http://www.finance.si/178684/|url-status=live}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |url=http://24ur.com/novice/slovenija/pahor-bi-kandidiral-za-predsednika.html |title=Pahor bi kandidiral za predsednika |access-date=2008-09-05 |publisher=24ur.com |date=4 March 2007 |language=sl |archive-date=12 October 2008 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20081012022040/http://24ur.com/novice/slovenija/pahor-bi-kandidiral-za-predsednika.html |url-status=live }}</ref> The Social Democrats had become the most popular party in opinion polls and were considered the likely winners at the next general election in 2008; opinion polls indicated that Pahor would easily win the presidential election.<ref>{{Cite web|url= http://24ur.com/bin/article.php?article_id=3098436&page=4&p_all_items=43|title= Pahor vodi v bitki za predsednika|access-date= 2008-09-05|publisher= 24ur.com|date= 26 May 2007|language= sl|archive-date= 18 January 2016|archive-url= https://web.archive.org/web/20160118075148/http://24ur.com/bin/article.php?article_id=3098436&page=4&p_all_items=43|url-status= live}}</ref> However, after months of mixed signals, Pahor finally announced that he would instead concentrate on the general election and would not run for the mostly ceremonial office of the president.<ref>{{Cite web|url= http://www.rtvslo.si/modload.php?&c_mod=rnews&op=sections&func=read&c_menu=1&c_id=145209|title= Pahor s SD-jem na parlamentarne volitve|access-date= 2008-09-05|publisher= Rtvslo.si|date= 22 June 2007|language= sl|archive-date= 9 November 2007|archive-url= https://web.archive.org/web/20071109125907/http://www.rtvslo.si/modload.php?&c_mod=rnews&op=sections&func=read&c_menu=1&c_id=145209|url-status= live}}</ref>

The Social Democrats then nominated [[Danilo Türk]], a former Slovenian ambassador and high official in the [[United Nations]], who at the time was a professor at the [[University of Ljubljana]]'s Faculty of Law.<ref>{{Cite web|url= http://www.rtvslo.si/modload.php?&c_mod=rnews&op=sections&func=read&c_menu=37&c_id=146955|title= Türk tudi uradno s podporo SD-ja|access-date= 2008-09-05|publisher= Rtvslo.si|date= 13 July 2007|language= sl|archive-date= 23 December 2007|archive-url= https://web.archive.org/web/20071223162021/http://www.rtvslo.si/modload.php?&c_mod=rnews&op=sections&func=read&c_menu=37&c_id=146955|url-status= live}}</ref> Türk's candidacy was also endorsed by [[Zares]]<ref>{{Cite web|url= http://www.finance.si/186521|title= Tudi v Zares bodo podprli Türka|access-date= 2008-09-05|publisher= Finance|date= 12 July 2007|language= sl|archive-date= 18 January 2016|archive-url= https://web.archive.org/web/20160118075148/http://www.finance.si/186521|url-status= live}}</ref> and the pensioners' party [[DeSUS]].<ref>{{Cite web |url=http://www.rtvslo.si/modload.php?&c_mod=rnews&op=sections&func=read&c_menu=37&c_id=150650 |title=DeSUS soglasno za Danila Türka |access-date=2008-09-05 |publisher=Rtvslo.si |date=27 August 2007 |language=sl |archive-date=2 May 2021 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210502110245/https://www.rtvslo.si/modload.php?c_mod=rnews&op=sections&func=read&c_menu=37&c_id=150650 |url-status=live }}</ref> Türk also gained support from [[Active Slovenia]] (AS) and the [[Party of Ecological Movements]] (SEG), two parties not represented in the National Assembly.<ref>{{Cite web |url=http://www.rtvslo.si/modload.php?&c_mod=rnews&op=sections&func=read&c_menu=37&c_id=148017 |title=Gaspari in Türk začenjata kampanjo |access-date=2008-09-05 |publisher=Rtvslo.si |date=26 June 2007 |language=sl |archive-date=2 May 2021 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210502110239/https://www.rtvslo.si/modload.php?c_mod=rnews&op=sections&func=read&c_menu=37&c_id=148017 |url-status=live }}</ref>

[[Liberal Democracy of Slovenia]] (LDS), which had earlier discussed the candidacy with Danilo Türk, subsequently nominated [[Mitja Gaspari]], the former Governor of the Bank of Slovenia. Gaspari had earlier had discussions with the Social Democrats about the candidacy.<ref name="Gaspari LDS">{{Cite web|url= http://www.rtvslo.si/modload.php?&c_mod=rnews&op=sections&func=read&c_menu=37&c_id=145876|title= Gaspari z LDS v predsedniško tekmo|access-date= 2008-09-05|publisher= Rtvslo.si|date= 30 June 2007|language= sl|archive-date= 2 May 2021|archive-url= https://web.archive.org/web/20210502110240/https://www.rtvslo.si/modload.php?c_mod=rnews&op=sections&func=read&c_menu=37&c_id=145876|url-status= live}}</ref>

The [[Slovenian National Party]] (SNS) nominated its leader, [[Zmago Jelinčič]].<ref>{{Cite web |url=http://www.finance.si/182731 |title=Jelinčič bi bil rad Drnovšek |access-date=2008-09-05 |publisher=Finance |date=25 May 2007 |language=sl |archive-date=18 January 2016 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20160118075148/http://www.finance.si/182731 |url-status=live }}</ref> Jelinčič had already run for the office at the [[2002 Slovenian presidential election|2002 election]], finishing third with 8.51% of the votes.<ref name="RVK"/>

Peterle, Türk and Gaspari all decided to enter the election as independent candidates and all managed to collect enough nominating votes with Peterle reaching the required number within the first 4 hours of the nominating process.<ref name="Peterle" /> Jelinčič was supported by his fellow party members.<ref name="Peterle">{{Cite web |url=http://www.zurnal24.si/cms/novice/volitve/index.html?id=8334 |title=Peterle in Jelinčič prva uradna kandidata |access-date=2008-09-05 |publisher=Žurnal24 |date=6 September 2007 |language=sl }}{{Dead link|date=April 2019 |bot=InternetArchiveBot |fix-attempted=yes }}</ref> Early polls indicated that Peterle, who had been campaigning for months and had cultivated the image of a "man of the people", would win the election in a runoff against Türk or possibly Gaspari.<ref>{{Cite web |url=http://24ur.com/bin/article.php?article_id=3107033 |title=Jelinčič najbolj napredoval |access-date=2008-09-05 |publisher=24ur.com |date=6 October 2007 |language=sl |archive-date=1 July 2012 |archive-url=https://archive.today/20120701102304/http://24ur.com/bin/article.php?article_id=3107033 |url-status=live }}</ref><ref>{{Cite web|url= http://www.robert-schuman.org/oee.php?num=469|title= Alojz Peterle and Danilo Türk to face each other in the second round of the presidential election in Slovenia on 11 November|access-date= 2008-11-02|publisher= The Robert Schuman Foundation|date= 21 October 2007|archive-date= 28 October 2007|archive-url= https://web.archive.org/web/20071028092938/http://www.robert-schuman.org/oee.php?num=469|url-status= live}}</ref>

