===Other candidates===
Other candidates, none of whom were expected to win a significant share of votes, were [[Darko Krajnc]] of the formerly parliamentarian [[Youth Party of Slovenia]], the disabled rights activist [[Elena Pečarič]], and [[Monika Piberl]], supported by the [[Women's Voice of Slovenia]] party.<ref>{{Cite web |url=http://24ur.com/novice/slovenija/zakaj-so-kandidirali.html |title=Zakaj so kandidirali? |access-date=2008-09-05 |publisher=24ur.com |date=21 August 2008 |language=sl |archive-date=20 May 2011 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20110520151139/http://24ur.com/novice/slovenija/zakaj-so-kandidirali.html |url-status=live }}</ref>
Pečarič was supported by non-aligned [[Majda Širca]], independent [[Slavko Gaber]] and [[Roberto Battelli]], representative of the Italian minority in Slovenia.<ref>{{Cite web |url=http://www.zurnal24.si/cms/novice/volitve/index.html?id=10653 |title=Pečaričeva peta kandidatka |access-date=2008-09-05 |publisher=Žurnal24 |date=20 September 2007 |language=sl }}{{Dead link|date=April 2019 |bot=InternetArchiveBot |fix-attempted=yes }}</ref> Krajnc and Piberl were supported by non-parliamentary political parties so they only needed to collect 3,000 support votes.<ref>{{Cite web|url= http://24ur.com/bin/article.php?article_id=3106207|title= Krajnc vložil kandidaturo|access-date= 2008-09-05|publisher= 24ur.com|date= 25 September 2007|language= sl|archive-date= 18 January 2016|archive-url= https://web.archive.org/web/20160118075148/http://24ur.com/bin/article.php?article_id=3106207|url-status= live}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web|url=http://www.finance.si/192314|title=Monika Piberl kot sedma v boj|access-date=2008-09-05|publisher=Finance|date=1 October 2007|language=sl|archive-date=18 January 2016|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20160118075148/http://www.finance.si/192314|url-status=live}}</ref>

Several other candidates publicly announced their intention to run for the office. Jože Andrejaš, Jožef Horvat, Matej Sedmak, Marjan Beranič, Marko Kožar and Pavel Premrl failed to gather sufficient public support or later decided to withdraw from the race.<ref>{{Cite web|url= http://www.zurnal24.si/cms/novice/volitve/index.html|title= Predsedniške volitve 2007|access-date= 2008-09-05|publisher= Žurnal24|language= sl|archive-url= https://web.archive.org/web/20080928031209/http://www.zurnal24.si/cms/novice/volitve/index.html|archive-date= 28 September 2008|url-status= dead}}</ref> Artur Štern, after leading a [[Parody|spoof]] campaign, announced that he was in fact performing a [[hidden camera]] experiment.<ref>{{Cite web|url= http://24ur.com/novice/slovenija/sternova-filmska-kampanja.html|title= Šternova filmska kampanja|access-date= 2008-09-05|publisher= 24ur.com|date= 13 September 2007|language= sl|archive-date= 11 February 2009|archive-url= https://web.archive.org/web/20090211013222/http://24ur.com/novice/slovenija/sternova-filmska-kampanja.html|url-status= live}}</ref> The footage was used by [[Franci Kek]] and [[Vojko Anzeljc]] in a film ''[[Gola resnica]]'', airing in early 2009 and addressing objectivity of media, problems with election legislation and reactions from Slovenes.<ref>{{Cite web|url= http://www.kolosej.si/filmi/film/gola-resnica/|title= Gola resnica|access-date= 2009-03-17|publisher= Kolosej.si|language= sl|archive-date= 16 February 2009|archive-url= https://web.archive.org/web/20090216143347/http://www.kolosej.si/filmi/film/gola-resnica/|url-status= live}}</ref>

==First round campaign==
The official election campaign began in late September 2007. The campaigns of the three front runners were based mostly on the personal appeal of the candidates, with few concrete statements about political issues. Zmago Jelinčič led an aggressive campaign, focusing on denouncing the three front runners, the Government, the ethnic and religious minorities, the Roman Catholic Church, and demanding an aggressive policy towards neighbouring [[Croatia]].<ref>{{Cite web |url=http://www.rtvslo.si/modload.php?&c_mod=rnews&op=sections&func=read&c_menu=37&c_id=152785 |title=Jelinčič: Slovenija potrebuje patriota |access-date=2008-09-05 |publisher=Rtvslo.si |date=20 September 2007 |language=sl |archive-date=2 May 2021 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210502110241/https://www.rtvslo.si/modload.php?c_mod=rnews&op=sections&func=read&c_menu=37&c_id=152785 |url-status=live }}</ref>

The candidates appeared in televised debates during which they discussed various topics. One of them were the rules governing the voting of non-resident nationals, which had been changed by the National Electoral Commission during the campaign. Before the campaign, non-resident nationals who wanted to cast their votes as [[absentee ballot]]s had been obliged to request voting materials, but the commission had introduced a new system in which such materials were sent to all non-residents entered in the electoral register, whether they had asked for them or not. Opposition parties, representing the left wing of Slovenian politics, disliked this move because the record of voters' addresses was not always reliable, and also because the rules had been changed after the campaign had already started.<ref>{{Cite web|url= http://www.dnevnik.si/novice/slovenija/327798|title= Opozicijski poslanci izpodbijajo določbo o glasovanju iz tujine|access-date= 2008-09-05|publisher= Dnevnik|date= 18 June 2007|language= sl|archive-date= 19 July 2011|archive-url= https://web.archive.org/web/20110719182025/http://www.dnevnik.si/novice/slovenija/327798|url-status= live}}</ref> They particularly opposed the change because voters from abroad seemed to favor right-wing parties, so that in the event of a very close ballot, votes from non-residents could tip the scale in favour of Peterle.<ref>{{Cite web |url=http://www.mladina.si/tednik/200742/clanek/slo--volitve-jure_trampus/ |title=Kaj bo, če bo tisoč glasov razlike? |access-date=2008-09-05 |website=Mladina |issue=2007/42 |language=sl |journal= |archive-date=24 September 2008 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20080924070544/http://www.mladina.si/tednik/200742/clanek/slo--volitve-jure_trampus/ |url-status=live }}</ref>

Other events overshadowed the campaign. During the summer, journalists Matej Šurc and Blaž Zgaga launched a ''Petition Against Censorship and Political Pressures on Journalists in Slovenia'', alleging government interference with journalism. The petition was signed by hundreds of Slovenian journalists from the mainstream media.<ref>{{Cite web |url=http://www.peticijazopercenzuro.com/ |title=Peticija zoper cenzuro |work=Matej Šurc, Blaž Zgaga |access-date=2008-09-09 |date=21 October 2007 |language=sl |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20080913094352/http://www.peticijazopercenzuro.com/ |archive-date=13 September 2008 |url-status=dead}}</ref> It was sent to the heads of state, prime ministers and parliamentary speakers of all EU member states during the campaign. Following the petition, the [[International Press Institute]] (IPI) sent a fact-finding mission to Ljubljana in November, to discuss the claims made in the petition with members of the Slovenian media. The contents of the mission's report remain confidential, but IPI called for the establishment of an independent commission to investigate the claims further.<ref>{{Cite web |url=http://www.freemedia.at/cms/ipi/freedom_detail.html?country=/KW0001/KW0003/KW0081/ |title=World Press Freedom Review |access-date=2008-09-10 |publisher=International Press Institute |year=2007 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20071012131115/http://www.freemedia.at/cms/ipi/freedom_detail.html?country=%2FKW0001%2FKW0003%2FKW0081%2F |archive-date=12 October 2007 |url-status=dead}}</ref>

Another event which attracted much debate was the Supreme Court's annulment of the 1946 war crimes conviction of [[Gregorij Rožman]]. Rožman was the Catholic bishop of Ljubljana who had been found guilty of war crimes and treason during [[World War II]] as a result of his collaboration with Italian and German occupation forces. Several attempts during the 1990s to review the trial had failed. This had led to Janša's government changing the law, enabling the religious communities to request a review of trials of their deceased members, something which had previously been reserved only for close relatives. After the [[Roman Catholic Archdiocese of Ljubljana|Archdiocese of Ljubljana]] initiated the review, the Supreme Court annulled the 1946 trial on procedural grounds, effectively rehabilitating Rožman, a decision that caused much controversy. This proved harmful for Peterle's campaign, as he was closely associated with the Catholic Church.{{refn|group=Note|One of the controversies during the short-lived centre-right government of [[Andrej Bajuk]]'s government was the participation of several leading members of the coalition in a ceremony in June 2000. The ceremony honored the members of the [[Slovene Home Guard]], a collaborationist militia, killed by the Communist regime in June 1945. At the ceremony, anthems of the Slovene Home Guard were sung. Among those attending were Prime Minister Andrej Bajuk, chairman of the National Assembly [[Janez Podobnik]], Defense Minister Janez Janša, Foreign Minister Lojze Peterle, and Archbishop of Ljubljana [[Franc Rode]].<ref>[[Sabrina Ramet]]: [http://www.wilsoncenter.org/index.cfm?topic_id=1422&fuseaction=topics.publications&doc_id=8506&group_id=7427 ''Slovenia since 1990''] {{Webarchive|url=https://web.archive.org/web/20061214223826/http://www.wilsoncenter.org/index.cfm?topic_id=1422&fuseaction=topics.publications&doc_id=8506&group_id=7427 |date=14 December 2006 }} Retrieved 2008-09-22.</ref>}} When asked about the Rožman case in a TV debate, Peterle confined himself to remarking that he was a supporter of the rule of law, that the war had divided the nation and that Rožman had played some part in that.<ref>{{Cite web|url= http://24ur.com/bin/article.php?article_id=3108074|title= Zadnje soočenje na POP TV|access-date= 2008-09-05|publisher= 24ur.com|date= 18 October 2007|language= sl|archive-date= 7 July 2012|archive-url= https://archive.today/20120707191746/http://24ur.com/bin/article.php?article_id=3108074|url-status= live}}</ref>

The last opinion polls published before the first round predicted a runoff between Peterle, who would win 40%, and either Türk or Gaspari. The latter were each predicted to receive 20–25%; most polls predicted a substantially larger share for Türk.<ref>{{Cite web|url= http://www.angus-reid.com/polls/view/28412/peterle_first_but_run_off_likely_in_slovenia|title= Peterle First, But runoff Likely in Slovenia|access-date= 2008-09-05|publisher= Angus Reid Global Monitor|date= 30 September 2007|archive-url= https://web.archive.org/web/20080331230321/http://www.angus-reid.com/polls/view/28412/peterle_first_but_run_off_likely_in_slovenia|archive-date= 31 March 2008|url-status= usurped}}</ref>

==First round result and reactions==
[[File:Slovenia pres 2007.png|right|thumb|300px|First round winners by electoral unit<br />
[[Lojze Peterle]]: 20–30% (pastel blue), 30–40% (dark blue)<br />
[[Mitja Gaspari]]: 20–30% (yellow)<br />
[[Zmago Jelinčič]]: 20–30% (orange)]]
The first round, held on 21 October, brought unexpected results. Contrary to predictions, Peterle won less than 29% of the vote, with Türk and Gaspari finishing a close second and third, respectively. Jelinčič, who according to opinion polls was expected to win around 12% of the vote, actually won almost 20%, finishing first in two of Slovenia's eight electoral units.<ref name="vp2007" />
Prime Minister Janez Janša blamed Peterle's poor showing on certain topics that were brought up during the campaign by "hidden centres of power". This was a reference to the journalists' petition, the timing of the Supreme Court's decision on the Rožman case and misinterpretation of Janša's and Minister of Economy [[Andrej Vizjak]]'s remarks on reasons for Slovenia's high inflation in 2007.<ref>{{Cite web|url= http://www.rtvslo.si/modload.php?&c_mod=rnews&op=sections&func=read&c_menu=37&c_id=155520|title= Janša upa na korekten drugi krog|access-date= 2008-09-05|publisher= Rtvslo.si|date= 23 October 2007|language= sl|archive-date= 25 December 2007|archive-url= https://web.archive.org/web/20071225102755/http://www.rtvslo.si/modload.php?&c_mod=rnews&op=sections&func=read&c_menu=37&c_id=155520|url-status= live}} ''Janša je tudi izpostavil, da so v zadnjih nekaj tednih različni vplivni centri iz ozadij sprožili teme, ki naj bi vplivale na izid. Med temi naj bi bile novinarska peticija, razveljavitev obsodbe škofu Rožmanu ter sprevračanje njegove izjave in izjave ministra Vizjaka o vzrokih za inflacijo.''</ref>

==Runoff campaign==
[[File:Glasovnica za predsednika republike 2007.jpg|300px|thumb|right|Runoff election ballot]]
Following the unexpected results of the first round, new opinion polls showed major changes, giving Türk a large lead over Peterle.<ref>{{Cite web|url= http://www.rtvslo.si/modload.php?&c_mod=rnews&op=sections&func=read&c_menu=37&c_id=155880|title= Ankete kažejo na zmago Türka|access-date= 2008-09-05|publisher= Rtvslo.si|date= 27 October 2007|language= sl|archive-date= 30 December 2007|archive-url= https://web.archive.org/web/20071230180220/http://www.rtvslo.si/modload.php?&c_mod=rnews&op=sections&func=read&c_menu=37&c_id=155880|url-status= live}}</ref> Liberal Democracy of Slovenia, which supported Gaspari in the first round, announced it would support Türk in the second.<ref>{{Cite web |url=http://24ur.com/novice/slovenija/t-rku-tudi-podpora-lds.html |title=Türku tudi podpora LDS |access-date=2008-09-05 |publisher=24ur.com |date=6 November 2007 |language=sl |archive-date=20 May 2011 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20110520151407/http://24ur.com/novice/slovenija/t-rku-tudi-podpora-lds.html |url-status=live }}</ref>

After the surprise gains of the flamboyant Jelinčič in the first round, the campaigns of both candidates opted for more concrete political statements in public campaigning and debates. Peterle replaced the head of his campaign, and concentrated on questioning Türk's role in the 1991 [[Dissolution of Yugoslavia|secession from Yugoslavia]]. Peterle alleged that at the time when he, as Prime Minister, struggled for Slovenia's independence, Türk continued to act as an official representative of Yugoslavia in international institutions.<ref name="fronta">{{Cite web |url=http://www.rtvslo.si/modload.php?&c_mod=rnews&op=sections&func=read&c_menu=37&c_id=156762 |title=Fronta na liniji Türkove vloge v 90. letih |access-date=2008-09-05 |publisher=Rtvslo.si |date=7 November 2007 |language=sl |archive-date=9 January 2008 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20080109232754/http://www.rtvslo.si/modload.php?&c_mod=rnews&op=sections&func=read&c_menu=37&c_id=156762 |url-status=live }}</ref> The campaign was backed by the Prime Minister Janša and the Foreign Minister [[Dimitrij Rupel]] who went so far as to confirm Peterle's claims on the Foreign Ministry's official website.<ref>{{Cite news |url=http://www.mladina.si/tednik/200745/clanek/slo-tema--jure_trampus/ |title=Pravi za zgago |access-date=2008-09-05 |publisher=Mladina |issue=2007/45 |language=sl |archive-date=2 May 2008 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20080502150447/http://www.mladina.si/tednik/200745/clanek/slo-tema--jure_trampus/ |url-status=live }}</ref> Türk denied the allegations, pointing to his opinion piece in the ''[[International Herald Tribune]]'' advocating international recognition of Slovenia, and the fact that it was Rupel himself who in 1992 appointed Türk as the Ambassador to the UN and praised him for his service to the country.<ref name="fronta" /><ref>{{Cite web|url= http://www.rtvslo.si/modload.php?&c_mod=rnews&op=sections&func=read&c_menu=37&c_id=156690|title= Türk: Kaže, da se je nekomu zameglil razum|access-date= 2008-09-05|publisher= Rtvslo.si|date= 6 November 2007|language= sl|archive-date= 3 January 2008|archive-url= https://web.archive.org/web/20080103020212/http://www.rtvslo.si/modload.php?&c_mod=rnews&op=sections&func=read&c_menu=37&c_id=156690|url-status= live}}</ref> Peterle's new strategy appeared to backfire, and the polls before the runoff predicted that Türk would win between 63% and over 70% of the vote.<ref>{{Cite web|url= http://www.b92.net/eng/news/region-article.php?yyyy=2007&mm=11&dd=09&nav_id=45234|title= Tuerk leads in Slovenia presidential runoff|access-date= 2008-09-05|publisher= B92|date= 9 November 2007|archive-url= https://web.archive.org/web/20110606220503/http://www.b92.net/eng/news/region-article.php?yyyy=2007&mm=11&dd=09&nav_id=45234|archive-date= 6 June 2011|url-status= dead}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web|url= http://www.angus-reid.com/polls/view/28934/tuerk_surges_ahead_in_slovenian_run_off|archive-url= https://archive.today/20120717200911/http://www.angus-reid.com/polls/view/28934/tuerk_surges_ahead_in_slovenian_run_off|url-status= usurped|archive-date= 17 July 2012|title= Tuerk Surges Ahead in Slovenian runoff|access-date= 2008-09-05|publisher= Angus Reid Global Monitor|date= 10 November 2007}}</ref>

==Runoff result and reactions==
The runoff was held on 11 November 2007. Exit poll results published at the closing of the vote predicted a victory for Türk, with 69% of the vote.<ref>{{Cite web |url=http://www.rtvslo.si/modload.php?&c_mod=rnews&op=sections&func=read&c_menu=37&c_id=157119 |title=Türku visoka zmaga za najvišji položaj |access-date=2008-09-05 |publisher=Rtvslo.si |date=7 November 2007 |language=sl |archive-date=15 January 2008 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20080115072942/http://www.rtvslo.si/modload.php?&c_mod=rnews&op=sections&func=read&c_menu=37&c_id=157119 |url-status=live }}</ref> "I'm very happy with the results as they appear now. I want to be a president who is uniting people. I believe that conditions here are such that people have a strong desire for something new," Türk told reporters.<ref name="France" />

Peterle conceded immediately. In his first statements, Peterle said his defeat was a vote against the ruling Janša government, and that he had expected a better result. He added, however, that he would have regretted it if he had not decided to run for the office.<ref>{{Cite web |url=http://24ur.com/novice/slovenija/ali-vas-je-prepricljiva-zmaga-danila-t-rka-presenetila.html |title=Delovati moramo kot ekipa |access-date=2008-11-02 |publisher=24ur.com |date=12 November 2007 |language=sl |archive-date=20 May 2011 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20110520151532/http://24ur.com/novice/slovenija/ali-vas-je-prepricljiva-zmaga-danila-t-rka-presenetila.html |url-status=live }}</ref> By midnight, unofficial results from the Electoral Commission gave Türk a lead of 68% vs. 32%. Türk won in all eight electoral units, with Peterle narrowly winning in only four of 88 electoral districts.<ref name="vp2007" /> Together with the second round of the election, a referendum on a law providing for the nationalization of citizens' share in the major national insurance company was held as well. Nearly three-quarters of the votes were cast against the law.<ref name="vp2007" />

The ballots from abroad that were considered potentially contentious before the election proved to have very little effect on the result. While Peterle received more votes from abroad than Türk, both the total number of votes and the difference was smaller than expected (3693 for Peterle and 3040 for Türk).<ref>{{Cite web|url= http://www.dnevnik.si/novice/slovenija/281752/|title= DVK je preštela glasovnice iz tujine, končni rezultati predsedniških volitev znani 27. novembra|access-date= 2009-03-24|publisher= dnevnik.si|date= 20 November 2007|language= sl|archive-date= 19 July 2011|archive-url= https://web.archive.org/web/20110719182412/http://www.dnevnik.si/novice/slovenija/281752/|url-status= live}}</ref>

Two days after the election, Prime Minister Janša announced that he might resign following what was perceived as a heavy defeat for the Government: "We will analyze the situation further, but all possibilities are open, including a resignation of the Government." He said that "it is particularly worrying that a lot of energy was invested in blackening the Government abroad," claiming his opponents portrayed Slovenia "as Belarus" or some other authoritarian country. The opposition parties said that talk of resignation just weeks before Slovenia took over [[Presidency of the Council of the European Union|European Union presidency]] was irresponsible and unwise,<ref>{{cite news|url= http://www.iht.com/articles/ap/2007/11/13/europe/EU-POL-Slovenia-Elections-Cabinet.php|title= Slovenia's PM: Cabinet might resign after opposition candidate elected president|access-date=2008-09-05 |newspaper= International Herald Tribune|date=13 November 2007 |quote=We will analyze the situation further, but all possibilities are open, including the resignation of the government," Jansa told reporters after a meeting of his center-right Cabinet. ... Jansa insisted that Tuerk's campaign "was directed entirely at settling accounts with the government." ... "It is particularly worrying that a lot of energy was invested in blackening the government abroad," Jansa said, claiming his opponents portrayed Slovenia "as Belarus" or some other authoritarian country.}}</ref> but the Prime Minister called a [[Motion of Confidence|vote of confidence]] for 19 November 2007.<ref>{{cite news |url=http://www.iht.com/articles/ap/2007/11/16/europe/EU-POL-Slovenia-Government.php |title=Slovenian PM seeks confidence vote after opposition candidate became president |access-date=2008-09-05 |newspaper=International Herald Tribune |date=16 November 2007 |archive-date=17 February 2008 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20080217044808/http://www.iht.com/articles/ap/2007/11/16/europe/EU-POL-Slovenia-Government.php |url-status=live }}</ref> The Government won the confidence vote, but support for the ruling SDS subsequently reached an all-time low, with only 18% of voters intending to vote for it in the [[2008 Slovenian parliamentary election|fall 2008 election]].<ref>{{Cite web|url=http://www.eubusiness.com/news-eu/1195512437.8 |title=Slovenian government survives confidence vote |access-date=2008-09-05 |publisher=EUbusiness.com |date=20 November 2007 |url-status=dead |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20090819145327/http://www.eubusiness.com/news-eu/1195512437.8 |archive-date=19 August 2009 }}</ref>

Reactions to Türk's victory from international media were positive. The Swiss newspaper ''[[Neue Zürcher Zeitung]]'' described him as "more or less the ideal man for the job".<ref>{{cite news|url= http://www.nzz.ch/nachrichten/international/in_slowenien_setzt_sich_der_aussenseiter_tuerk_durch_1.583508.html|title= In Slowenien setzt sich der Aussenseiter Türk durch|access-date= 2008-09-16|newspaper= Neue Zürcher Zeitung|date= 12 November 2007|language= de|archive-date= 17 February 2008|archive-url= https://web.archive.org/web/20080217040556/http://www.nzz.ch/nachrichten/international/in_slowenien_setzt_sich_der_aussenseiter_tuerk_durch_1.583508.html|url-status= live}}''Als einer der wenigen slowenischen Politiker mit internationaler Bekanntheit galt er in Hinblick auf Sloweniens nahende EU-Präsidentschaft als Idealbesetzung.''</ref> The media focused on the landslide victory that was perceived to be a severe defeat for Janša's centre-right coalition.<ref name="France">{{Cite web|url= http://www.france24.com/france24Public/en/archives/news/world/20071111-slovenia-presidential-election-danilo-Turk-european-union.php|title= Turk elected president of Slovenia|access-date= 2008-09-16|publisher= France 24|date= 11 November 2007|quote= I'm very happy with the results as they appear now. I want to be a president who is uniting people. I believe that conditions here are such that people have a strong desire for something new," Turk told reporters after exit polls were shown. ... "To the EU I would say: Slovenia is your solid, faithful and credible partner. Rely on us, and we'll be a good president of the European Union next year," he said.|archive-url= https://web.archive.org/web/20120312143506/http://www.france24.com/france24Public/en/archives/news/world/20071111-slovenia-presidential-election-danilo-Turk-european-union.php|archive-date= 12 March 2012|url-status= dead}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web|url= http://afp.google.com/article/ALeqM5gdT41sCQXeM3f0WLKGzFa6Qf984A|title= Danilo Turk wins Slovenia presidential vote|access-date= 2008-09-16|publisher= Agence France-Presse|date= 11 November 2007|archive-url= https://web.archive.org/web/20110520111957/http://afp.google.com/article/ALeqM5gdT41sCQXeM3f0WLKGzFa6Qf984A|archive-date= 20 May 2011|url-status= dead}}</ref> Since the EU presidency was closing, Türk's diplomatic background was put forward. "Slovenia is your solid, faithful and credible partner. Rely on us, and we'll be a good president of the European Union next year," Türk said.<ref name="France" /> Türk was also expected to maintain Slovenia's alliance with the United States even though he was highly critical of the [[war in Iraq]], as [[Al Jazeera Media Network|Al Jazeera]] reported.<ref>{{Cite web|url= http://english.aljazeera.net/news/europe/2007/11/2008525142946599747.html|title= Ex-UN diplomat wins Slovenia polls|access-date= 2008-09-16|publisher= Al Jazeera|date= 12 November 2007|archive-date= 5 June 2011|archive-url= https://web.archive.org/web/20110605024915/http://english.aljazeera.net/news/europe/2007/11/2008525142946599747.html|url-status= live}}</ref>

On 22 December, Türk was sworn in as the President of the Republic of Slovenia. In his inaugural address, he thanked his predecessor Janez Drnovšek for his contribution to success and respect of Slovenia. Later, he also stated that he would work closely with Janša's government during Slovenia's six-month EU presidency.<ref>{{Cite web|url= http://www.rtvslo.si/modload.php?&c_mod=rnews&op=sections&func=read&c_menu=1&c_id=160710|title= Türk prisegel kot predsednik države|access-date= 2008-09-09|publisher= Rtvslo.si|date= 22 December 2007|language= sl|archive-date= 25 December 2007|archive-url= https://web.archive.org/web/20071225105620/http://www.rtvslo.si/modload.php?&c_mod=rnews&op=sections&func=read&c_menu=1&c_id=160710|url-status= live}}</ref>

Janez Drnovšek would die a little over three months after the election from kidney cancer on 23 February 2008, at the age of 57.

